#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-add(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:7
msgid "git-add - Add file contents to the index"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\tgit add [--verbose | -v] [--dry-run | -n] [--force | -f] [--interactive | "
"-i] [--patch | -p]\n"
"\t       [--edit | -e] [--[no-]all | -A | --[no-]ignore-removal | [--update "
"| -u]] [--sparse]\n"
"\t       [--intent-to-add | -N] [--refresh] [--ignore-errors] "
"[--ignore-missing] [--renormalize]\n"
"\t       [--chmod=(+|-)x] [--pathspec-from-file=<file> "
"[--pathspec-file-nul]]\n"
"\t       [--] [<pathspec>...]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:22
msgid ""
"Add contents of new or changed files to the index. The \"index\" (also known "
"as the \"staging area\") is what you use to prepare the contents of the next "
"commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:26
msgid ""
"When you run `git commit` without any other arguments, it will only commit "
"staged changes. For example, if you've edited `file.c` and want to commit "
"your changes to that file, you can run:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:29
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"   git add file.c\n"
"   git commit\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:31
msgid "You can also add only part of your changes to a file with `git add -p`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:36
msgid ""
"This command can be performed multiple times before a commit.  It only adds "
"the content of the specified file(s) at the time the add command is run; if "
"you want subsequent changes included in the next commit, then you must run "
"`git add` again to add the new content to the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:39
msgid ""
"The `git status` command can be used to obtain a summary of which files have "
"changes that are staged for the next commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:44
msgid ""
"The `git add` command will not add ignored files by default. You can use the "
"`--force` option to add ignored files. If you specify the exact filename of "
"an ignored file, `git add` will fail with a list of ignored files. Otherwise "
"it will silently ignore the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:47
msgid ""
"Please see linkgit:git-commit[1] for alternative ways to add content to a "
"commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:50
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:51
#, no-wrap
msgid "`<pathspec>...`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:63
msgid ""
"Files to add content from.  Fileglobs (e.g. `*.c`) can be given to add all "
"matching files.  Also a leading directory name (e.g. `dir` to add "
"`dir/file1` and `dir/file2`) can be given to update the index to match the "
"current state of the directory as a whole (e.g.  specifying `dir` will "
"record not just a file `dir/file1` modified in the working tree, a file "
"`dir/file2` added to the working tree, but also a file `dir/file3` removed "
"from the working tree). Note that older versions of Git used to ignore "
"removed files; use `--no-all` option if you want to add modified or new "
"files but ignore removed ones."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:66
msgid ""
"For more details about the _<pathspec>_ syntax, see the 'pathspec' entry in "
"linkgit:gitglossary[7]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:67
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-n`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--dry-run`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:71
msgid ""
"Don't actually add the file(s), just show if they exist and/or will be "
"ignored."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:72
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-v`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--verbose`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:75
msgid "Be verbose."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-f`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:77
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--force`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:79
msgid "Allow adding otherwise ignored files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--sparse`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:86
msgid ""
"Allow updating index entries outside of the sparse-checkout cone.  Normally, "
"`git add` refuses to update index entries whose paths do not fit within the "
"sparse-checkout cone, since those files might be removed from the working "
"tree without warning. See linkgit:git-sparse-checkout[1] for more details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:87
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-i`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--interactive`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:93
msgid ""
"Add modified contents in the working tree interactively to the "
"index. Optional path arguments may be supplied to limit operation to a "
"subset of the working tree. See ``Interactive mode'' for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-p`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:95
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--patch`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:100
msgid ""
"Interactively choose hunks of patch between the index and the work tree and "
"add them to the index. This gives the user a chance to review the difference "
"before adding modified contents to the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:104
msgid ""
"This effectively runs `add --interactive`, but bypasses the initial command "
"menu and directly jumps to the `patch` subcommand.  See ``Interactive mode'' "
"for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-e`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:108
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--edit`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:112
msgid ""
"Open the diff vs. the index in an editor and let the user edit it.  After "
"the editor was closed, adjust the hunk headers and apply the patch to the "
"index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:118
msgid ""
"The intent of this option is to pick and choose lines of the patch to apply, "
"or even to modify the contents of lines to be staged. This can be quicker "
"and more flexible than using the interactive hunk selector.  However, it is "
"easy to confuse oneself and create a patch that does not apply to the "
"index. See EDITING PATCHES below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-u`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:120
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--update`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:124
msgid ""
"Update the index just where it already has an entry matching _<pathspec>_.  "
"This removes as well as modifies index entries to match the working tree, "
"but adds no new files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:129
msgid ""
"If no _<pathspec>_ is given when `-u` option is used, all tracked files in "
"the entire working tree are updated (old versions of Git used to limit the "
"update to the current directory and its subdirectories)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:130
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-A`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--all`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-ignore-removal`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:137
msgid ""
"Update the index not only where the working tree has a file matching "
"_<pathspec>_ but also where the index already has an entry. This adds, "
"modifies, and removes index entries to match the working tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:142
msgid ""
"If no _<pathspec>_ is given when `-A` option is used, all files in the "
"entire working tree are updated (old versions of Git used to limit the "
"update to the current directory and its subdirectories)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:143
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-all`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:144
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--ignore-removal`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:149
msgid ""
"Update the index by adding new files that are unknown to the index and files "
"modified in the working tree, but ignore files that have been removed from "
"the working tree.  This option is a no-op when no _<pathspec>_ is used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:153
msgid ""
"This option is primarily to help users who are used to older versions of "
"Git, whose `git add <pathspec>...` was a synonym for `git add --no-all "
"<pathspec>...`, i.e. ignored removed files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:154
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-N`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:155
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--intent-to-add`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:161
msgid ""
"Record only the fact that the path will be added later. An entry for the "
"path is placed in the index with no content. This is useful for, among other "
"things, showing the unstaged content of such files with `git diff` and "
"committing them with `git commit -a`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:162
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--refresh`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:165
msgid ""
"Don't add the file(s), but only refresh their stat()  information in the "
"index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:166
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--ignore-errors`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:172
msgid ""
"If some files could not be added because of errors indexing them, do not "
"abort the operation, but continue adding the others. The command shall still "
"exit with non-zero status.  The configuration variable `add.ignoreErrors` "
"can be set to true to make this the default behaviour."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:173
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--ignore-missing`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:178
msgid ""
"This option can only be used together with `--dry-run`. By using this option "
"the user can check if any of the given files would be ignored, no matter if "
"they are already present in the work tree or not."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:179
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-warn-embedded-repo`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:185
msgid ""
"By default, `git add` will warn when adding an embedded repository to the "
"index without using `git submodule add` to create an entry in "
"`.gitmodules`. This option will suppress the warning (e.g., if you are "
"manually performing operations on submodules)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:186
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--renormalize`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:194
msgid ""
"Apply the \"clean\" process freshly to all tracked files to forcibly add "
"them again to the index.  This is useful after changing `core.autocrlf` "
"configuration or the `text` attribute in order to correct files added with "
"wrong _CRLF/LF_ line endings.  This option implies `-u`. Lone CR characters "
"are untouched, thus while a _CRLF_ cleans to _LF_, a _CRCRLF_ sequence is "
"only partially cleaned to _CRLF_."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:195
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--chmod=(+|-)x`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:199
msgid ""
"Override the executable bit of the added files.  The executable bit is only "
"changed in the index, the files on disk are left unchanged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:200
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--pathspec-from-file=<file>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:207
msgid ""
"Pathspec is passed in _<file>_ instead of commandline args. If _<file>_ is "
"exactly `-` then standard input is used. Pathspec elements are separated by "
"_LF_ or _CR/LF_. Pathspec elements can be quoted as explained for the "
"configuration variable `core.quotePath` (see linkgit:git-config[1]). See "
"also `--pathspec-file-nul` and global `--literal-pathspecs`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:208
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--pathspec-file-nul`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:212
msgid ""
"Only meaningful with `--pathspec-from-file`. Pathspec elements are separated "
"with _NUL_ character and all other characters are taken literally (including "
"newlines and quotes)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:213
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:217
msgid ""
"This option can be used to separate command-line options from the list of "
"files, (useful when filenames might be mistaken for command-line options)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:220
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:224
msgid ""
"Adds content from all ++*.txt++ files under `Documentation` directory and "
"its subdirectories:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:227
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git add Documentation/\\*.txt\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:232
msgid ""
"Note that the asterisk ++*++ is quoted from the shell in this example; this "
"lets the command include the files from subdirectories of `Documentation/` "
"directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:234
msgid "Considers adding content from all ++git-*.sh++ scripts:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:237
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git add git-*.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:242
msgid ""
"Because this example lets the shell expand the asterisk (i.e. you are "
"listing the files explicitly), it does not consider `subdir/git-foo.sh`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:244
#, no-wrap
msgid "INTERACTIVE MODE"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:248
msgid ""
"When the command enters the interactive mode, it shows the output of the "
"'status' subcommand, and then goes into its interactive command loop."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:253
msgid ""
"The command loop shows the list of subcommands available, and gives a prompt "
"\"What now> \".  In general, when the prompt ends with a single '>', you can "
"pick only one of the choices given and type return, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:259
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    *** Commands ***\n"
"      1: status       2: update       3: revert       4: add untracked\n"
"      5: patch        6: diff         7: quit         8: help\n"
"    What now> 1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:263
msgid ""
"You also could say `s` or `sta` or `status` above as long as the choice is "
"unique."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:265
msgid "The main command loop has 6 subcommands (plus help and quit)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:266
#, no-wrap
msgid "status"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:273
msgid ""
"This shows the change between `HEAD` and index (i.e. what will be committed "
"if you say `git commit`), and between index and working tree files "
"(i.e. what you could stage further before `git commit` using `git add`) for "
"each path.  A sample output looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:278
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"              staged     unstaged path\n"
"     1:       binary      nothing foo.png\n"
"     2:     +403/-35        +1/-1 add-interactive.c\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:289
msgid ""
"It shows that `foo.png` has differences from `HEAD` (but that is binary so "
"line count cannot be shown) and there is no difference between indexed copy "
"and the working tree version (if the working tree version were also "
"different, 'binary' would have been shown in place of 'nothing').  The other "
"file, `add-interactive.c`, has 403 lines added and 35 lines deleted if you "
"commit what is in the index, but working tree file has further modifications "
"(one addition and one deletion)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:290
#, no-wrap
msgid "update"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:299
msgid ""
"This shows the status information and issues an \"Update>>\" prompt.  When "
"the prompt ends with double '>>', you can make more than one selection, "
"concatenated with whitespace or comma.  Also you can say ranges.  E.g. \"2-5 "
"7,9\" to choose 2,3,4,5,7,9 from the list.  If the second number in a range "
"is omitted, all remaining patches are taken.  E.g. \"7-\" to choose 7,8,9 "
"from the list.  You can say '*' to choose everything."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:302
msgid "What you chose are then highlighted with '*', like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:307
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"           staged     unstaged path\n"
"  1:       binary      nothing foo.png\n"
"* 2:     +403/-35        +1/-1 add-interactive.c\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:311
msgid "To remove selection, prefix the input with `-` like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:314
#, no-wrap
msgid "Update>> -2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:318
msgid ""
"After making the selection, answer with an empty line to stage the contents "
"of working tree files for selected paths in the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:319
#, no-wrap
msgid "revert"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:324
msgid ""
"This has a very similar UI to 'update', and the staged information for "
"selected paths are reverted to that of the HEAD version.  Reverting new "
"paths makes them untracked."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:325
#, no-wrap
msgid "add untracked"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:329
msgid ""
"This has a very similar UI to 'update' and 'revert', and lets you add "
"untracked paths to the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:330
#, no-wrap
msgid "patch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:337
msgid ""
"This lets you choose one path out of a 'status' like selection.  After "
"choosing the path, it presents the diff between the index and the working "
"tree file and asks you if you want to stage the change of each hunk.  You "
"can select one of the following options and type return:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:354
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"y - stage this hunk\n"
"n - do not stage this hunk\n"
"q - quit; do not stage this hunk or any of the remaining ones\n"
"a - stage this hunk and all later hunks in the file\n"
"d - do not stage this hunk or any of the later hunks in the file\n"
"g - select a hunk to go to\n"
"/ - search for a hunk matching the given regex\n"
"j - go to the next undecided hunk, roll over at the bottom\n"
"J - go to the next hunk, roll over at the bottom\n"
"k - go to the previous undecided hunk, roll over at the top\n"
"K - go to the previous hunk, roll over at the top\n"
"s - split the current hunk into smaller hunks\n"
"e - manually edit the current hunk\n"
"p - print the current hunk\n"
"P - print the current hunk using the pager\n"
"? - print help\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:357
msgid ""
"After deciding the fate for all hunks, if there is any hunk that was chosen, "
"the index is updated with the selected hunks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:360
msgid ""
"You can omit having to type return here, by setting the configuration "
"variable `interactive.singleKey` to `true`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:361
#, no-wrap
msgid "diff"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:365
msgid "This lets you review what will be committed (i.e. between `HEAD` and index)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:368
#, no-wrap
msgid "EDITING PATCHES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:378
msgid ""
"Invoking `git add -e` or selecting `e` from the interactive hunk selector "
"will open a patch in your editor; after the editor exits, the result is "
"applied to the index. You are free to make arbitrary changes to the patch, "
"but note that some changes may have confusing results, or even result in a "
"patch that cannot be applied.  If you want to abort the operation entirely "
"(i.e., stage nothing new in the index), simply delete all lines of the "
"patch. The list below describes some common things you may see in a patch, "
"and which editing operations make sense on them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:380
#, no-wrap
msgid "added content"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:384
msgid ""
"Added content is represented by lines beginning with \"{plus}\". You can "
"prevent staging any addition lines by deleting them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:385
#, no-wrap
msgid "removed content"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:389
msgid ""
"Removed content is represented by lines beginning with \"-\". You can "
"prevent staging their removal by converting the \"-\" to a \" \" (space)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:390
#, no-wrap
msgid "modified content"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:397
msgid ""
"Modified content is represented by \"-\" lines (removing the old content)  "
"followed by \"{plus}\" lines (adding the replacement content). You can "
"prevent staging the modification by converting \"-\" lines to \" \", and "
"removing \"{plus}\" lines. Beware that modifying only half of the pair is "
"likely to introduce confusing changes to the index."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:405
msgid ""
"There are also more complex operations that can be performed. But beware "
"that because the patch is applied only to the index and not the working "
"tree, the working tree will appear to \"undo\" the change in the index.  For "
"example, introducing a new line into the index that is in neither the `HEAD` "
"nor the working tree will stage the new line for commit, but the line will "
"appear to be reverted in the working tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:407
msgid "Avoid using these constructs, or do so with extreme caution."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:409
#, no-wrap
msgid "removing untouched content"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:415
msgid ""
"Content which does not differ between the index and working tree may be "
"shown on context lines, beginning with a \" \" (space).  You can stage "
"context lines for removal by converting the space to a \"-\". The resulting "
"working tree file will appear to re-add the content."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:416
#, no-wrap
msgid "modifying existing content"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:423
msgid ""
"One can also modify context lines by staging them for removal (by converting "
"\" \" to \"-\") and adding a \"{plus}\" line with the new content.  "
"Similarly, one can modify \"{plus}\" lines for existing additions or "
"modifications. In all cases, the new modification will appear reverted in "
"the working tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:424
#, no-wrap
msgid "new content"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:429
msgid ""
"You may also add new content that does not exist in the patch; simply add "
"new lines, each starting with \"{plus}\". The addition will appear reverted "
"in the working tree."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:433
msgid ""
"There are also several operations which should be avoided entirely, as they "
"will make the patch impossible to apply:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:435
msgid "adding context (\" \") or removal (\"-\") lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:436
msgid "deleting context or removal lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-add.adoc:437
msgid "modifying the contents of context or removal lines"
msgstr ""

